Svitlana writes the number $147$ on a blackboard. Then, at any point, if the number on the blackboard is $n$, she can perform one of the following three operations:

\begin{itemize}
\item if $n$ is even, she can replace $n$ with $\frac{n}{2}$;
\item if $n$ is odd, she can replace $n$ with $\frac{n+63}{2}$; and
\item if $n \geq 32$, she can replace $n$ with $n-32$.
\end{itemize}

Compute the number of possible values that Svitlana can obtain by doing zero or more operations.

The following analog clock has two hands that can move independently of each other.

Initially, both hands point to the number 0 (the top of the clock). The clock performs a sequence of hand movements so that on each movement, one of the two hands moves clockwise to the next number on the clock while the other hand does not move.

Let $N$ be the number of sequences of $100$ hand movements such that during the sequence, every possible positioning of the hands appears exactly once, and at the end of the $100$ movements, the hands have returned to their initial position. Find the remainder when $N$ is divided by $1000$.

[asy]
unitsize(2cm);
draw(unitcircle,black+linewidth(2));

// tick marks for a 10‑hour clock
for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    draw(0.9*dir(36*i)--dir(36*i));
}
// thicker tick marks at the "12 o\'clock" and "6 o\'clock" positions
for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i) {
    draw(0.85*dir(180*i)--dir(180*i),black+linewidth(2));
}
// numbers around the dial
for (int i = 0; i < 10; ++i) {
    label("\small" + (string) i, dir(90 - i * 36) * 0.75);
}
// two hands (drawn as arrows pointing upward)
draw((0,0)--0.6*dir(90),black+linewidth(2),Arrow(TeXHead,2bp));
draw((0,0)--0.4*dir(90),black+linewidth(2),Arrow(TeXHead,2bp));
[/asy]

Suppose $E, I, L, V$ are (not necessarily distinct) nonzero digits in base ten for which

\begin{itemize}
\item the four-digit number $\underline{E} \underline{V} \underline{I} \underline{L}$ is divisible by $71$, and
\item the four-digit number $\underline{V} \underline{I} \underline{L} \underline{E}$ is divisible by $73$.
\end{itemize}

Compute the four-digit number $\underline{L} \underline{I} \underline{V} \underline{E}$.

Find the largest prime number $p<2000$ for which there exists a complex number $z$ satisfying
\begin{itemize}
 \item the real and imaginary part of $z$ are both integers;
 \item $|z|=\sqrt{p}$, and
 \item there exists a triangle whose three side lengths are $p$, the real part of $z^{3}$, and the imaginary part of $z^{3}$.
\end{itemize}
